WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.895
BRENT SHAFFER: Come,
everybody, gather round.

00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:11.870
I'm going to tell you a
wonderful, magical tale,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:17.100
a tale of two Google employees
who set off on a bold journey

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.810
to create an entirely serverless
application on Google Cloud.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.710
Some of these events
might frighten you,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.170
but you're going to learn
a lot about Google Cloud

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:33.140
and maybe even a little
bit about yourself.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.020
So first the dream.

00:00:38.062 --> 00:00:39.520
So if you've been
around this area,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.520
you'll see a lot of
these experiments.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.980
What you may not know
is these experiments

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.850
are also available online.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.530
And you can go to
showcase.withgoogle.com,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.460
and you can launch these
apps and mess with them

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.610
in your own browser.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.242
You can also browse the
code and download it

00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:01.200
and launch it yourself.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.060
My friend Sandeep, who
you may see him around.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.350
He's also a Cloud Googler.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.970
Him and I had an idea to make
this app, a montage maker.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.750
And what a montage
maker is is it

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.740
something that will
create a video that's

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.448
assembled from clips of
words from another video that

00:01:27.448 --> 00:01:29.740
have been reordered to say
or show something different.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.510
So for instance, if you
uploaded a political speech,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.267
then you could
make, potentially,

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.100
by rearranging the words
said in that video,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.930
you could make the politician
sing the Pokemon theme song,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.010
for example.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:49.260
So let's see what
this looks like.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.030
We decided to take
a couple of videos

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:58.543
from DevRelers from Cloud
Next 2018 and have them sing--

00:01:58.543 --> 00:01:59.210
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:01:59.210 --> 00:01:59.550
- Never going--

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.150
BRENT SHAFFER: Rick Astley.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.410
- --to give you up.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.027
Never going to let you down.

00:02:04.027 --> 00:02:04.610
[END PLAYBACK]

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.180
BRENT SHAFFER: OK.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:05.940
Let me do that one more time.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.960
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.120
- Never going to give you up.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.050
Never going to let you down.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:12.810
[END PLAYBACK]

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.320
BRENT SHAFFER: So not
exactly a deep fake,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.010
but you get the idea.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.130
So what this consists
of is a back end

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.040
which will take a video
and it will separate it

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.310
into individual word
clips for each word

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.090
and a front end that
will take a text input

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.370
and it will assemble
that text into videos

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.687
using the clips that we
split from the back end.

00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:37.020
Now, what makes this serverless?

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.478
Well, nothing makes
this serverless

00:02:38.478 --> 00:02:40.190
except how we decided
to architect it.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.730
So we wanted to keep scalability
in mind at the forefront

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.470
so that it can scale
up automatically

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.820
and it scales down to 0
when we're not using it.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.610
We also wanted it
to be event based.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:55.120
So nothing is running until
it's spun up by something.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.300
And we wanted to have our code
broken apart into microservices

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.520
and also have
minimal data at rest.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.920
So let's go to a demo.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.380
Hi, Google Cloud Next.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.290
I really hope this demo works.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.190
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.170
- Hi, Google Cloud Next.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.540
I really hope this demo works.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.848
[END PLAYBACK]

00:03:28.848 --> 00:03:30.160
BRENT SHAFFER: OK.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:30.780
Good enough.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:31.743
Thank you.

00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:33.192
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.070
All right, so let's talk
about how we built it.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.990
The front end is
pretty straightforward.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:43.580
Not really all that
much serverless

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.900
going on here anyway.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.610
We have a front end being served
by Firebase hosting, which

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:53.090
is a really easy way to
deploy a simple HTML app.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.660
And in JavaScript, we call a
back end basically functioning

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:02.450
as an API, which is a Cloud
Function listening on a HTTP

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.770
trigger.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.670
And that Cloud Function
takes a text string

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:10.580
and looks and sees if those
clips exist in cloud storage.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.750
And if they do,
it combines them.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.900
And if it doesn't, it
fills in the missing words

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.029
with the Text-to-Speech
API, which

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:21.980
is what you saw with
the Cloud Platform logo

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.360
in the previous demo.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.200
So a back end that
isn't serverless

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.430
might look something like this.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.450
And this is your
code sitting on, say,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.550
Compute Engine, a VM, whatever.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.200
You give it a video.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.110
It uses video
intelligence API, which

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:46.740
can give you the start and end
times for every word and using

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.760
that information, cut up
the video into word clips.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.840
But since we want to go
serverless, for starters,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.490
we decided that we use a cloud
storage bucket to initialize

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:00.990
the Cloud Function.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.120
So when you drop a video
into a bucket and it

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.350
spins up our Cloud Function.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.450
But we can do one better,
because the video API, Video

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:14.880
Intelligence API, can save the
results of the start and end

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.420
time of every word into
JSON file in cloud storage.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.860
So now we can make
that asynchronous.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.010
We're not waiting on our
Video Intelligence API

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.220
to process our video anymore.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.950
We just sent off
the API request.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.180
That function dies.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.610
And then once the
Video Intelligence API

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:37.290
is finished processing,
a new called function

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:42.960
is triggered, which then
splits the word into clips.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.240
So now we have three
storage buckets

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.023
on the back end, one storage
bucket on the front end.

00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:49.440
And at this point
if you're going,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.220
I thought this was supposed
to be a happy ending story,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.180
you're not alone.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.503
So this is pretty complicated.

00:05:57.503 --> 00:05:59.670
If anyone told you that
Cloud Functions is something

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.210
that you just run your
code and you walk away

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.627
and you don't have to worry
about servers or anything else

00:06:03.627 --> 00:06:04.680
anymore, then they lied.

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.050
But before we get
too discouraged,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.360
let me show you what it
looks like, because everybody

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.750
loves a good demo.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.960
So here are our cloud
storage buckets.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.713
I have a video
here from my good--

00:06:22.713 --> 00:06:23.380
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.040
- My name is Felipe Hoffa.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.173
You're interested
in visualizing data.

00:06:27.173 --> 00:06:28.562
Prepare to rewind.

00:06:28.562 --> 00:06:29.718
[END PLAYBACK]

00:06:29.718 --> 00:06:31.510
BRENT SHAFFER: My good
friend Felipe Hoffa,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.280
who is also a Googler.

