yep.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:52.862
We have all of our videos.

00:07:52.862 --> 00:07:53.850
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:54.350
- Rewind.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:58.800
Interested.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:07:59.490
[END PLAYBACK]

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.735
BRENT SHAFFER: OK, awesome.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.550
So just to recap, we use
Cloud Function functions

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.810
for all the glue code.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.390
We use Cloud Storage
for storing our data

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:16.060
and also for triggering
additional functions.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.520
We use the Video Intelligence
API for identifying words

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:19.570
in the video.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.840
We use the Test-to-Speech API
for filling in missing words.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:27.250
And Pub/Sub maybe?

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.950
Other technologies
used, just for fun.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:31.670
We use Firebase
Hosting for serving

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.530
HTML, NodeJS for
the function code,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:39.400
FFmpeg for any audio or video
manipulation nerds out there.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.620
We used Firebase CLI to actually
deploy the functions, which

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.190
it's a really nice interface.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.020
It's a really great
way to get started.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.500
We use Cloud Source Repositories
for version control and Cloud

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.930
Shell Editor and Cloud
Shell for editing the code,

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:54.370
running the tests,
and deploying.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:58.480
So we are fully integrated
into the cloud with this one.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.390
So let's talk about the bugs.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.180
Then the bugs came.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.800
So the first issue we ran into
was the Video Intelligence API

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:08.860
is just--

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.870
it's really not that precise.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.770
So the precision is
1/10 of a second, which

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.050
if you're talking as
quickly as I am now

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.630
or as most people
speak in conversation,

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:23.000
that's not enough to really
get an isolated word.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.900
So to get around that, we
did a couple of things.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.700
We added a second buffer and
used the fade in and fade out,

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:30.640
which did help.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.057
But at the end of
the day, we just

00:09:32.057 --> 00:09:34.852
had to approve each word,
which is a horrible process,

00:09:34.852 --> 00:09:37.060
but it's still better than
going through and clipping

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.710
these words out yourself.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:41.030
And we did this
with an admin UI.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:45.190
I'll show this real
quickly, just for fun.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.480
Probably one in every
five words is good.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:47.980
That's how--

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.647
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:09:48.647 --> 00:09:49.210
- Kubernetes.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:49.420
[END PLAYBACK]

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:50.530
BRENT SHAFFER:
Unprecise this is.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:51.197
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:09:51.197 --> 00:09:52.610
- Landscape.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.100
Laptop.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:54.680
The launch.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:55.290
[END PLAYBACK]

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.320
BRENT SHAFFER: So the
longer words are better.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.953
Shorter words like the and this
and a, the real common ones

00:10:00.953 --> 00:10:02.120
are really difficult to get.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.280
Now, these are the
more fun issues.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.040
So we started by uploading
these five minute videos.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.210
They got chopped
up about as quickly

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.790
as you saw the demo here.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:17.950
And we thought, this is great.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.410
We architected this
thing to scale.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.220
We're feeling really
good about ourselves.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.520
Then we tried a 60 minute video.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:28.860
And can anybody guess
what went wrong?

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.382
You saw the
architectural diagram.

00:10:32.382 --> 00:10:33.340
We didn't guess either.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.592
But this actually
seems really obvious.

00:10:36.592 --> 00:10:38.050
So the first thing
that happened is

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:40.720
we hit maximum execution time.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.970
Cloud Function can only
run for nine minutes,

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:46.480
and we're having it chop up
60 minutes worth of words.

00:10:49.683 --> 00:10:51.600
So you remember, we have
a Cloud Function fire

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.650
off once the annotations
land in Cloud Storage.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.750
How do we fix it?

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.670
I foreshadowed it.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:01.720
Pub/Sub.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:02.900
Yes, Pub/Sub.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:04.150
Pub/Sub is the hero we needed.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.163
Pub/Sub is built
for scalability.

00:11:06.163 --> 00:11:07.080
It's highly available.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:08.620
It's global.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.330
It's awesome.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:12.760
So we stuck it right here.

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:16.840
We had every single
function, every single word

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:20.950
spin up a Pub/Sub message to
be handled by another function.

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:24.585
We added an additional Cloud
Function specifically just

00:11:24.585 --> 00:11:25.460
to clip the words up.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.570
So you're seeing
microservices in play.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:32.505
We're cutting this application
up into the tiniest of pieces.

00:11:32.505 --> 00:11:34.380
Can anyone tell me what
went wrong with this?

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:39.790
Maximum number of
parallel functions.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:44.350
So a 60 minute video
of someone talking

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:48.580
has 10,000 words maybe,
something around there.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:50.440
6,000 words.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:52.750
All being processed
at the same time

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.368
with a function
for each of them.

00:11:54.368 --> 00:11:55.660
So here we got a little greedy.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.740
We went too far down
the distribution path.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.230
And as a result of having
too many functions,

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.550
we then had too many outstanding
Pub/Sub messages as well,

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:07.300
and then what happens
if you have too many

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:08.508
outstanding Pub/Sub messages?

00:12:08.508 --> 00:12:09.320
They get dropped.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.250
So we were just having
words that were lost.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.710
This had a pretty easy solution.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:15.880
We just added batching.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:19.120
So instead of having every
single function process one

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.560
word, we had every function
spin up and process 10 words.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:27.250
We probably could've gone as
far as a couple hundred words,

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:29.500
because we initially
only had one function,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.070
and it was processing
five minutes of video.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.820
So this is another one
of those trade offs

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:38.500
where it will get done faster
the more of these Cloud

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.670
Functions you spin up.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:44.530
But then you could hit
some of these limits.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.380
So we immediately
hit the CPU quota

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:52.720
exceeded, because let's
say we had 10,000 words.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:59.050
Now we're spinning up close
to 1,000 functions a second.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:00.790
And each of them are
processing 10 words,

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:03.050
which probably takes a minute.

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:07.550
So we're hitting this CPU quota.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:11.680
Now, we could get around this
by pausing after so many words,

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.780
only processing
so many at a time.

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:15.580
What we did was
we just requested

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:18.460
that our quota got raised,
because a lot of these quotas

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:21.950
are configurable.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.610
And that worked.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.810
So that was nice.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.380
And I'm really glad we
got this error next,

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.010
because it meant that we got the
grand slam of Cloud Functions

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:33.670
errors.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:38.530
We hit maximum execution time,
maximum number of threads,

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.920
maximum CPU, and maximum memory.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.210
So I guess we're
doing something right.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:45.760
Now, a Cloud
Function can only be

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:47.830
configured for two
gigabytes of memory,

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.210
and that is a hard limit.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.980
So when we hit this, we knew
we had to fix something.

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:53.590
Luckily, it was a
bug in our code.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:57.380
We had to configure
FFmpeg to use streams

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:00.175
so it wasn't operating on
an entire 60 minute video.

00:14:00.175 --> 00:14:02.470
And there was another
issue that we had here

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:08.110
where we were storing the 60
minute video in a temp file

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:09.280
and not cleaning it up.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.860
And that's an issue
that I'm going

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.450
to talk about in a second.

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.145
So some Cloud
Functions operations,

00:14:16.145 --> 00:14:17.770
optimizations out
there that you really

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:20.353
do need to keep in mind whenever
you're using Cloud Functions,

00:14:20.353 --> 00:14:23.973
is each function spins
up an instance specific

00:14:23.973 --> 00:14:24.640
to its function.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.680
So you have a Function A
and Function B. Function

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:28.097
A is going to spin
up a Function A

00:14:28.097 --> 00:14:31.020
instance that can handle
multiple Function A

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.920
invocations.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:37.720
So in our case, when we
had these functions that

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.180
were processing a 60
minute video, each of them

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:45.100
were pulling down a 60 minute
video file, storing it in temp,

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:48.470
and in Cloud Functions
the file system is memory.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:51.100
So you're filling up your
two gigabytes of memory.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.920
And we weren't cleaning
it up, and those files

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.640
stuck around until the function
ran on memory and crashed.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:59.890
What we did, we can actually
use that to our advantage

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:02.500
and have every function check
and see if the video was

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:03.490
already downloaded.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:05.620
And now you skip
having to download

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:06.850
that video for each function.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.750
So you can use the temp
directory and the globals

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:15.040
in Cloud Functions
as a caching system.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:20.880
Another optimization
is function size.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.320
So if you use Firebase
CLI, it's really easy

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.178
because it just takes
an app, a singular app,

00:15:27.178 --> 00:15:28.970
and it deploys it to
every single function.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:30.740
And this is really
great to get started,

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:33.410
but once your
application matures,

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:36.140
you're going to want
to split each function

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.900
to its own application
so that you don't

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:41.300
have a function deploying
with another function's

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:42.360
dependencies.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.300
So in our case, our
annotate video function,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.260
which all it does is call
the Video Intelligence API,

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:52.160
it was loading in 300
megabytes of dependencies

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:55.010
that were being used in some of
the more complex functions that

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.080
use FFmpeg.

00:15:57.080 --> 00:15:58.760
So keep that in mind.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:02.250
If you use Firebase CLI, this is
an optimization you might want.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:05.570
In the G Cloud, you can
also deploy functions

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:06.720
with the G Cloud CLI.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:08.490
That's one of the
advantages it has.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:11.390
It's a little bit
harder to use, but it

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:15.840
does give you the ability to
deploy individual code bases.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:17.190
So denouement.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:18.560
Let's resolve this story.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.355
Our takeaway was building
scalable serverless apps is--

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:28.880
it wasn't terrible.

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:30.840
It wasn't great.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:32.465
It's just a very
different paradigm

00:16:32.465 --> 00:16:33.590
and one we weren't used to.

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:35.840
So be prepared for that.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.260
You have different challenges.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.815
You're going to run into
some big frustrations.

00:16:40.815 --> 00:16:42.440
I'm going to go
through the cons first,

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.320
because I want to
end on a high note.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.470
First one we already
talked about.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:52.230
Seemingly simple apps
get very complex.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:55.472
If we weren't serverless,
this is a pretty good solution

00:16:55.472 --> 00:16:56.930
for what we wanted
on our back end.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.965
And it might actually
work just as well.

00:16:59.965 --> 00:17:01.340
And this is what
we came up with.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:03.140
This is actually the flow.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:04.240
And this looks insane.

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:06.619
If someone was like,
hey, here's our app,

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:09.026
you're gonna have to debug
this, and they hand me

00:17:09.026 --> 00:17:11.359
a piece of paper that looked
like that, I would be like,

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:13.232
you guys, you messed this up.

00:17:13.232 --> 00:17:13.940
This isn't right.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.384
As a result, debugging
is really hard.

00:17:19.384 --> 00:17:23.983
So you can watch the logs
for function invocations,

00:17:23.983 --> 00:17:25.400
but at the end of
the day, there's

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:31.820
no way to see an event go
from start to finish easily.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:34.380
And I know we have
products like Cloud Trace

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.380
and we might be able to
integrate that in there.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.520
Didn't have time to try that.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.998
So it may be possible
in the future,

00:17:40.998 --> 00:17:43.040
but currently there's
nothing out of the box that

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:43.957
allows you to do this.

00:17:43.957 --> 00:17:47.060
So you're stuck looking at
logs and refreshing storage

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:49.400
buckets, which is not--
it's a little more

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:51.080
difficult to follow.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:55.190
And another thing is serverless
abstracts away your code

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:56.660
from the hardware,
and that's true.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.910
So you don't have to worry
about hardware limitations,

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:02.240
but instead you have
quota limitations, which

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.310
can be just as frustrating.

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:07.010
So let's talk about some
good things, though.

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:10.715
The app forced us to
think about microservices.

00:18:10.715 --> 00:18:12.590
And as a result, you
get a lot of good things

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:13.550
with microservices.

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:18.410
For instance, the fact that
each function is hermetic.

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:20.270
Deploying a new one
is quick, and it

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:24.680
won't affect any of the other
running components of your app.

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:28.280
Also quote exceptions
were indicators of flaws

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:31.110
in our design every time.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:34.730
With the exception of the
CPU quota that we had raised,

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:37.880
we were able to get around the
quotas by better architecture

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:38.792
of our app.

00:18:38.792 --> 00:18:41.000
And it forced us to think
about things that we hadn't

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:43.850
previously thought about.

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:45.950
Quota increases
also, in general,

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:48.830
are better than adding
hardware, especially

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:51.290
if you haven't architected
your application

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:53.180
to scale with new hardware.

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.500
You just build it on a single
Compute Engine instance,

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.292
you can add another
Compute Engine instance

00:18:57.292 --> 00:19:00.290
and it's not going
to help you at all.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:02.840
Also, there was very little
code required in our apps.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:07.880
This entire application had
under 300 lines of JavaScript,

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:10.700
and that was when I just looked
backstage before this talk.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.310
I didn't remove white space
or comments or logging

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:14.230
or any of that stuff.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:18.860
So it's relatively
small, easy to maintain.

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:21.930
Another really good aspect
to this is the cost.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:24.050
So we talked about
scaling down to 0.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:27.840
We left the app inactive
for several weeks,

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:33.350
and the total cost was 0.1
dollar a day, one cent a day.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:35.870
Best cent a day I
ever spent on tech.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:37.940
And this was just
to house the videos

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:40.010
that we had in cloud storage.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:41.760
Our most expensive day
during development,

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:44.690
this was when we were throwing
all sorts of videos at it

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:46.970
and we were blowing
through quotas

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:49.840
and we had tons
of inefficiencies

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:51.740
and things that were
costing us extra money

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:53.330
was still only $100.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:57.080
And the total cost for the
project over two months

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:58.310
was about $200.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:02.470
So if you were running all
of this on a Compute Engine

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:06.290
instance and you really
feel that you made it really

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:09.720
beefy so that it could hold
all this, it would be about $50

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:10.220
a month.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:11.580
So it'd be pretty cheap.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.880
But whenever you're
not using it,

00:20:13.880 --> 00:20:15.600
you're still paying
that same price.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:18.460
So it's going to catch up.

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.985
So was it worth the
trouble of serverless?

00:20:20.985 --> 00:20:22.570
I don't know.

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:26.240
I guess that's for
you guys to decide.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.460
Thank you.

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

